            <ArticleTitle>Topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for corneal abrasions in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>748-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="QUESTION" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Corneal abrasion from minor injury to the eye is common in both adults and children. Some of my colleagues prescribe topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) for analgesia. How safe is this practice?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="ANSWER" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Topical ophthalmic NSAIDs are a short-term effective treatment of the pain associated with corneal abrasions in children. Rare but serious complications have been reported in adult case-study series. Children with corneal abrasions should have follow-up appointments within 24 to 48 hours to assess healing, complications, and side effects of treatment, particularly if they are using topical NSAIDs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
